.TH dd 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
dd \- copy a file, converting as it goes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dd
[\fIoption\fB=\fIvalue\fR] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dd
copies from one file to another with a chosen block size, optionally
converting the data on the way.  Everything is an
.IB option = value
pair; there are no dashes.
.PP
It is the tool for talking to devices, where the size of a read matters:
copying a boot sector, writing an image to a partition, reading a fixed
number of blocks from the middle of something.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI if= file
Read from
.I file
rather than standard input.
.TP
.BI of= file
Write to
.I file
rather than standard output.
.TP
.BI ibs= n
Input block size, 512 by default.
.TP
.BI obs= n
Output block size.
.TP
.BI bs= n
Both at once.  When input and output block sizes are the same, the data
is copied straight through with no reblocking, which is faster and is
what you want for a device.
.TP
.BI cbs= n
Conversion buffer size, for the
.B ascii
and
.B ebcdic
conversions.
.TP
.BI skip= n
Skip
.I n
input blocks before starting.
.TP
.BI seek= n
Skip
.I n
output blocks before starting to write.
.TP
.BI count= n
Copy only
.I n
input blocks.
.TP
.BI files= n
Copy
.I n
files, for a tape.
.TP
.BI conv= keyword ,...
One or more of:
.B ascii
convert EBCDIC to ASCII,
.B ebcdic
the other way,
.B ibm
the other way with a different table,
.B lcase
fold to lower case,
.B ucase
fold to upper case,
.B swab
swap every pair of bytes,
.B noerror
carry on after a read error,
.B sync
pad every input block out to the input block size.
.SH SIZES
A number may be followed by
.B k
for kilobytes,
.B b
for 512-byte blocks or
.B w
for words, and two numbers joined by
.B x
are multiplied.
.SH OUTPUT
.B dd
reports on standard error how many whole and partial blocks it read and
wrote.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# dd if=/dev/hda of=mbr.bin bs=512 count=1
# dd if=image.bin of=/dev/hdb3 bs=4k
# dd if=/dev/hda2 of=/dev/null bs=8k     read the whole partition
.fi
.SH NOTES
.B of=
truncates nothing and seeks nothing by itself: writing a short file over
a longer one leaves the tail of the old one in place.  That is what
makes
.B dd
right for patching part of a device and wrong as a general copy \- use
.BR cp (1)
for files.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cp (1),
.BR bd (1),
.BR od (1),
.BR mkfs (1)
